AB  - Iron is an essential micronutrient for fungal pathogens, and its acquisition is tightly regulated to ensure survival in environments with limited iron, such as those found within hosts. The transcription factor HapX plays a central role in maintaining iron homeostasis and regulating adaptive responses during iron deprivation in fungi. In this study, we investigated HapX's global regulatory functions through transcriptomic profiling, ChIP-seq analysis, and immunodetection. Silencing HapX resulted in reduced fungal growth and decreased virulence under iron-limiting conditions, both in vitro and in vivo. Transcriptomic analysis of the silenced strain revealed significant upregulation of genes involved in metal transport (zinc, copper, and calcium) and cell wall remodeling, indicating that HapX normally represses these genes, which become derepressed upon its silencing. ChIP-seq analysis confirmed HapX binding to the promoters of key genes involved in metal ion regulation and metabolic adaptation. This finding underscores HapX's dual role as a transcriptional activator and repressor. Taken together, our findings establish HapX as a regulator of fungal physiology under iron-restricted conditions and highlight the HapX-iron axis as a promising target for antifungal intervention.
LA  - eng
UR  - http://europepmc.org/abstract/MED/41377739
